---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const moneyCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'gap', label: 'output gap (recession − / boom +)', min: -5, max: 5, step: 0.5, value: -2, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'Taylor-rule rate = 2 + inflation + 0.5·gap − 1', expr: '2 + 2 + 0.5*gap - 1', unit: '%' },
    { label: 'rate move suggested per meeting', expr: 'min(0.5, max(-0.5, 0.25*gap))', unit: 'pp' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/monetary-policy-central-banks">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Central banks steer the economy with <strong>interest rates</strong>. Raise rates: borrowing
    costs climb, spending cools, inflation eases. Cut rates: credit flows, spending rises,
    employment recovers. Their mandate in most rich countries: <strong>price stability (~2%
    inflation) plus maximum employment</strong> — and their main tools are the policy rate,
    balance-sheet operations (quantitative easing), and the credibility of their word.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Taylor rule — the famous recipe mapping the economy's temperature into an interest rate" widget="sliderLab" config={moneyCfg} />

  <p>
    The Taylor rule (1993): set the rate near 2% (neutral) plus inflation, tightened by 0.5
    points for each point of overheating. It describes what central banks roughly do — and gives
    markets a benchmark to be surprised by. When inflation ran to 9% in 2022, the rule said
    raise fast; the Fed did.
  </p>

  <h2>How the tools work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The policy rate cascades:</strong> central bank rate → interbank lending → mortgages, loans, savings. Transmission takes 12–18 months — "monetary policy acts with long and variable lags" (Friedman).</li>
    <li><strong>Quantitative easing:</strong> when rates hit zero (2008, 2020), central banks bought long bonds to push down longer-term rates — expanding balance sheets from $900B (2008) to ~$9T (2022) at the Fed.</li>
    <li><strong>Expectations are half the fight:</strong> workers and firms set wages and prices on <em>expected</em> inflation. Central bank credibility — built by hitting targets — makes disinflation cheaper. "Talk is policy" (forward guidance).</li>
    <li><strong>The trade-offs are real:</strong> tightening fights inflation by causing unemployment; easing risks asset bubbles and currency slides. Volcker's 1980 rates near 20% killed US inflation at the cost of a deep recession — the field's reference example of costly credibility-building.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Taylor (1993); Friedman, "The Role of Monetary Policy" (1968); Bernanke, The Federal Reserve and the Financial Crisis.</p>
</TopicPage>
